I see! For `ASN1_ETYPE_OCTET_STRING`, `asn1_read_value` calls `asn1_get_octet_der` which decodes the DER-encoded OCTET STRING and returns the raw value. So `tmp` contains the raw value (without the DER tag and length).

Then `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` is called with this raw value. But `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` expects the input to be a DER-encoded string (with tag and length). So there's a mismatch!

Wait, but this is how the code works. Let me look at the `_gnutls_x509_read_string` function again:

```c
result = _gnutls_x509_decode_string(etype, tmp, slen, ret, allow_ber);
```

`etype` is the type passed to `_gnutls_x509_read_string`. In the case of `_decode_pkcs12_auth_safe`, `etype = ASN1_ETYPE_OCTET_STRING`. So `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` is called with `ASN1_ETYPE_OCTET_STRING` and `tmp` which is the raw value of the OCTET STRING.

But `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` calls `asn1_decode_simple_ber(ASN1_ETYPE_OCTET_STRING, tmp, slen, ...)`. This function expects `tmp` to be a BER-encoded OCTET STRING (with tag and length). But `tmp` is the raw value, not a BER-encoded value.

So `asn1_decode_simple_ber` would try to parse the first byte of `tmp` as a tag. If the first byte happens to be 0x04 (OCTET STRING tag), it would parse the next bytes as the length and then the content. But if the first byte is something else, it would fail.

Wait, but this is the same for all string types. Let me check if `asn1_decode_simple_ber` with `DECODE_FLAG_HAVE_TAG` actually expects a tag:

Looking at `_asn1_decode_simple_ber`:
```c
if (dflags & DECODE_FLAG_HAVE_TAG) {
    result = asn1_get_tag_der(p, der_len, &class, &tag_len, &tag);
    ...
    p += tag_len;
    der_len -= tag_len;
    ...
}

result = asn1_get_length_der(p, der_len, &len_len);
...
p += len_len;
der_len -= len_len;
...
```

So with `DECODE_FLAG_HAVE_TAG`, it expects a tag and length. Without it, it expects just the length.

But `asn1_decode_simple_ber` always passes `DECODE_FLAG_HAVE_TAG`. So it expects the input to have a tag.

But `asn1_read_value` for `ASN1_ETYPE_OCTET_STRING` returns the raw value without the tag. So there's a mismatch.

